@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Joins Introduction";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE TITLE -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Joins Introduction
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL Server JOINs are used to combine related data from
            two or more tables based on a related column.
        </p>

        <p>
            In real-world databases, data is usually divided into
            multiple tables. JOINs allow us to retrieve meaningful
            information by combining data from those tables.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS A JOIN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a JOIN?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A JOIN is an SQL operation used to combine rows from
            two or more tables based on a related column between
            those tables.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, suppose we have a
            <strong>Students</strong> table and a
            <strong>Courses</strong> table. Both tables may have
            a common column such as <strong>CourseId</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We can use a JOIN to display the student name together
            with the course name.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- WHY JOINS ARE USED -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Why Do We Use JOINs?
        </h2>

        <p>
            JOINs are important because related information is often
            stored in different tables instead of keeping everything
            in a single table.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To combine data from multiple tables.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To retrieve related information.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To avoid unnecessary duplicate data.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To work with normalized database structures.
            </li>

            <li>
                To create meaningful reports and results.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- EXAMPLE TABLES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Tables
        </h2>

        <p>
            Consider the following two tables.
        </p>

        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            Students Table
        </h4>

        <div class="table-responsive mb-4">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>CourseId</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>1</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>2</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>1</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>3</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>


        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-3">
            Courses Table
        </h4>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>CourseId</th>
                        <th>CourseName</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>1</td>
                        <td>.NET Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>2</td>
                        <td>Java Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>3</td>
                        <td>Python Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMMON COLUMN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Related Column
        </h2>

        <p>
            In our example, the common column is
            <strong>CourseId</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
            The Students table stores CourseId to identify the
            course selected by the student, while the Courses table
            contains the actual course information.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>Students.CourseId = Courses.CourseId</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- BASIC JOIN SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic JOIN Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT columns
FROM Table1
JOIN Table2
ON Table1.CommonColumn = Table2.CommonColumn;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The <strong>ON</strong> condition specifies how the
            records in the two tables are related.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- SIMPLE EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Simple JOIN Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            To display student names along with their course names,
            we can join the Students and Courses tables.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    Students.StudentName,
    Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
JOIN Courses
    ON Students.CourseId = Courses.CourseId;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- RESULT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Result
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>CourseName</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>.NET Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>Java Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>.NET Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>Python Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- TYPES OF JOINS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Types of JOINs
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides different types of JOINs for
            retrieving related data in different ways.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>JOIN Type</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>INNER JOIN</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns matching records from both tables.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>LEFT JOIN</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns all records from the left table
                            and matching records from the right table.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>RIGHT JOIN</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns all records from the right table
                            and matching records from the left table.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>FULL OUTER JOIN</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns matching and non-matching records
                            from both tables.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>CROSS JOIN</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Returns every possible combination of
                            rows from both tables.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    JOINs are mainly used to combine related data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The ON condition defines the relationship between
                    tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Tables commonly have a primary key and foreign key
                    relationship.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Different JOIN types produce different result sets.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- KEY POINTS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Key Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    JOIN combines data from two or more tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A common column is generally used to relate tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The ON clause defines the JOIN condition.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL OUTER and CROSS JOIN
                    are covered in this module.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The correct JOIN should be selected according to
                    the required result.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned what SQL JOINs are, why
                JOINs are used, how tables are related using common
                columns, and the basic JOIN syntax. We also introduced
                the major JOIN types that will be covered in the
                upcoming sections.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


